<section id="controls">
  <h2>The controls</h2>

  <h3>Repository selector</h3>
  <p>The dashboard shows one repository at a time, because delivery health is a property of a codebase and its pipeline, not of an average across them. Pick a repository from the selector in the top right; the whole page reloads for it. The repository's name is the page heading, so it's always obvious what you're looking at.</p>

  <h3>Time window</h3>
  <p>Three windows: <strong>30d</strong>, <strong>90d</strong> and <strong>6m</strong>. Ninety days is the default: long enough to see a trend, short enough to reflect how the team works now.</p>
  <p>A larger window only becomes selectable once you have more history than the window below it, so a chart can never imply six months of data from three weeks of it. As your history grows, the wider windows unlock on their own.</p>

  <h3>Freshness indicator</h3>
  <p>Underneath the repository name: a dot, when metrics were last recomputed, and how many days of data exist. Green means the numbers are current; anything else means the recompute hasn't run recently and you should treat the trends as slightly behind. Full detail is on <a href="/docs/data-quality">Data quality</a>.</p>

  <h3>Profile menu</h3>
  <p>Top right. Switch or create workspaces and, if you're an owner, reach the <strong>Team</strong>, <strong>Repositories</strong> and <strong>Deployment Tracking</strong> settings.</p>
</section>

<section id="key-metrics">
  <h2>Key metrics</h2>
  <p>The top row answers "how are we doing right now?" in five numbers. Each reflects the selected repository and time window, except the open-PR figures, which are always current state.</p>

  <div class="docs-rows">
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Deployment Frequency (deploys / week)</strong>
      <p>Production ships per week across the window.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Lead Time (median: merge to production)</strong>
      <p>How long merged code waits before users get it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>PR Cycle Time (median: PR open to merge)</strong>
      <p>How long the review-and-iterate loop takes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Throughput (PRs / engineer / month)</strong>
      <p>Merged pull requests, normalised by the number of distinct authors in the window, so the number doesn't jump when the team grows or shrinks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Open PRs (live and draft)</strong>
      <p>How much work is in flight right now, split so early-stage drafts don't inflate the review queue.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>A card reading <strong>"Requires a connected production environment"</strong> isn't an error: that repository tracks deployments but has no environment Distilled recognises as production. See <a href="/docs/deployment-tracking">Deployment tracking</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="trends">
  <h2>Trends</h2>
  <p>Four charts answer "and is that getting better or worse?". Each has an <strong>info</strong> button explaining what it plots.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deployment Frequency</strong>: deployments per day across the window. Look for consistency as much as height; a flat line of small ships beats a spike before a quiet fortnight.</li>
    <li><strong>Lead Time</strong>: weekly median and 75th percentile in hours. The gap between the two lines is your tail. A low median with a high P75 means most changes ship quickly and some get stuck.</li>
    <li><strong>PR Cycle Time</strong>: weekly median and P75, read the same way.</li>
    <li><strong>PR Ageing</strong>: the age distribution of pull requests open right now. A healthy repository is weighted towards the youngest bucket.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Headline numbers are computed live over exactly the window you selected; the weekly chart series are recomputed on a schedule. That's why a chart can lag a headline figure by up to an hour.</p>
</section>

<section id="reading-it">
  <h2>Reading it well</h2>
  <p>Distilled is built to be glanced at for thirty seconds rather than studied. A few habits make that reliable:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read the pair, not the number.</strong> Cycle time with throughput. Deployment frequency with lead time. Either one alone can be improved in ways nobody wants.</li>
    <li><strong>Trust the trend before the value.</strong> The absolute median depends on how your team splits work; the direction over six weeks doesn't.</li>
    <li><strong>Check data quality before acting on a surprise.</strong> A sudden improvement is often a pipeline change, not a team change.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't compare engineers.</strong> These are pipeline metrics. Throughput per engineer describes the system's capacity to move work, not anyone's contribution.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

